<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    EMPTY ON PURPOSE, and the reasoning is load-bearing (2026-10-26).

    This file carried `keychain-access-groups = D8UWYY2KY7.md.myne.desktop` for
    the MYND-329 quick-unlock Touch ID provider. That value is the app's DEFAULT
    access group under a `D8UWYY2KY7.md.myne.desktop` signature, and the provider
    never asks for a group (`core/src/plugins/quick_unlock/apple.rs` sets only
    `kSecAccessControlBiometryCurrentSet` and `kSecAttrAccessibleWhenUnlockedThisDeviceOnly`,
    with no `kSecAttrAccessGroup`), so the entitlement granted nothing the app did
    not already have.

    What it did cost was the launch. On macOS 26 a restricted entitlement is only
    honored with a provisioning profile that authorizes it, and with the profile
    embedded and matching the certificate the spawn was still refused
    (`Launchd job spawn failed`, NSPOSIXErrorDomain 163). Signed without the
    entitlement, the same bundle launches, and `security-framework`'s macOS
    SecItem path uses the classic keychain with the default group.

    The earlier note here said the entitlement was required or biometric writes
    return errSecMissingEntitlement (-34018); that is true of the DATA PROTECTION
    keychain, which this code does not opt into on macOS.

    If biometric quick-unlock ever fails with -34018 on a real device, the answer
    is a Developer ID provisioning profile with Keychain Sharing plus this key
    back, not a wider entitlement: `myne-ops/runbooks/local-multiplatform-build.md`.
  -->
</dict>
</plist>
